{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-123269" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y),$ traversed along the line segment from $(1,3)$ to $(0,3)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/state.json" "file_sha256" "15804d37d758b6bbacaa2be8fc51b62cf1efd972fcbc52534e7ab0fff05a5402"} {"question" "q-123269" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(1,3)$ to $(0,3).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=i+3\\,j$ and $b=3\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(i+3\\,j)+t[3\\,j-(i+3\\,j)] \\\\  & =(i+3\\,j)-t\\,i \\\\  & =(1-t)\\,i+3\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=1-t$ and $y=3$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3)j \\\\  & =-i \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((1-t)+3) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-t)\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/state.json" "file_sha256" "15804d37d758b6bbacaa2be8fc51b62cf1efd972fcbc52534e7ab0fff05a5402"} {"question" "q-123269" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c1adca5610002853d484ecc9e3d63043a2df88e9516d60c468a45c409ff532ac"} {"question" "q-123269" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/state.json" "file_sha256" "15804d37d758b6bbacaa2be8fc51b62cf1efd972fcbc52534e7ab0fff05a5402"} {"question" "q-123269" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-2t)\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-t)\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-t)\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-2t)\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-t)\\,\\text{d}t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/state.json" "file_sha256" "15804d37d758b6bbacaa2be8fc51b62cf1efd972fcbc52534e7ab0fff05a5402"} {"question" "q-123269" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-t)\\,\\text{d}t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/state.json" "file_sha256" "15804d37d758b6bbacaa2be8fc51b62cf1efd972fcbc52534e7ab0fff05a5402"} {"question" "q-123283" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y)={e}^{x+y},$ traversed along the line segment from $(0,0)$ to $(-3,4)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/state.json" "file_sha256" "15804d37d758b6bbacaa2be8fc51b62cf1efd972fcbc52534e7ab0fff05a5402"} {"question" "q-123283" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(0,0)$ to $(-3,4).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=0$ and $b=-3\\,i+4\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =0+t\\,(b-0) \\\\  & =t(b) \\\\  & =t(-3\\,i+4\\,j) \\\\  & =-3t\\,i+4t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=-3t$ and $y=4t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-3t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t)j \\\\  & =-3\\,i+4\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={e}^{x+y}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & ={e}^{-3t+4t} \\\\  & ={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\cdot 5\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/state.json" "file_sha256" "15804d37d758b6bbacaa2be8fc51b62cf1efd972fcbc52534e7ab0fff05a5402"} {"question" "q-123283" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c1adca5610002853d484ecc9e3d63043a2df88e9516d60c468a45c409ff532ac"} {"question" "q-123283" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/state.json" "file_sha256" "15804d37d758b6bbacaa2be8fc51b62cf1efd972fcbc52534e7ab0fff05a5402"} {"question" "q-123283" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "10{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "2\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{7t}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "2\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "5{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{7t}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "5{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/state.json" "file_sha256" "15804d37d758b6bbacaa2be8fc51b62cf1efd972fcbc52534e7ab0fff05a5402"} {"question" "q-123283" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/state.json" "file_sha256" "15804d37d758b6bbacaa2be8fc51b62cf1efd972fcbc52534e7ab0fff05a5402"} {"question" "q-123268" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=1+x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,$ traversed along the line segment from $(-1,1)$ to $(1,2)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/state.json" "file_sha256" "15804d37d758b6bbacaa2be8fc51b62cf1efd972fcbc52534e7ab0fff05a5402"} {"question" "q-123268" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(-1,1)$ to $(1,2).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=-i+j$ and $b=i+2\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(-i+j)+t[(i+2\\,j)-(-i+j)] \\\\  & =(-i+j)+t(2\\,i+j) \\\\  & =(2t-1)\\,i+(t+1)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=2t-1$ and $y=t+1$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-1)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+1)j \\\\  & =2\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=1+x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =1+(2t-1)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1) \\\\  & =2t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1))\\cdot \\sqrt{5}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1))\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/state.json" "file_sha256" "15804d37d758b6bbacaa2be8fc51b62cf1efd972fcbc52534e7ab0fff05a5402"} {"question" "q-123268" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c1adca5610002853d484ecc9e3d63043a2df88e9516d60c468a45c409ff532ac"} {"question" "q-123268" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/state.json" "file_sha256" "15804d37d758b6bbacaa2be8fc51b62cf1efd972fcbc52534e7ab0fff05a5402"} {"question" "q-123268" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "5{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1))\\text{d}t"] ["math" "5{\\int }_{0}^{1}(t-1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)\\text{d}t"] ["math" "\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1))\\text{d}t"] ["math" "\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(4t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\text{d}t"] ["math" "\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(t-1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)\\text{d}t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/state.json" "file_sha256" "15804d37d758b6bbacaa2be8fc51b62cf1efd972fcbc52534e7ab0fff05a5402"} {"question" "q-123268" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1))\\text{d}t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/state.json" "file_sha256" "15804d37d758b6bbacaa2be8fc51b62cf1efd972fcbc52534e7ab0fff05a5402"} {"question" "q-123127" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-y,$ traversed along the line segment from $(0,2)$ to $(3,1)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/state.json" "file_sha256" "15804d37d758b6bbacaa2be8fc51b62cf1efd972fcbc52534e7ab0fff05a5402"} {"question" "q-123127" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(0,2)$ to $(3,1).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=2\\,j$ and $b=3\\,i+j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =2\\,j+t[(3\\,i+j)-2\\,j] \\\\  & =2\\,j+t(3\\,i-j) \\\\  & =3t\\,i+(2-t)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=3t$ and $y=2-t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2-t)j \\\\  & =3\\,i-j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(3t)^{2}-(2-t) \\\\  & =9{t}^{2}+t-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{2}+t-2)\\cdot \\sqrt{10}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{2}+t-2)\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/state.json" "file_sha256" "15804d37d758b6bbacaa2be8fc51b62cf1efd972fcbc52534e7ab0fff05a5402"} {"question" "q-123127" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c1adca5610002853d484ecc9e3d63043a2df88e9516d60c468a45c409ff532ac"} {"question" "q-123127" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/state.json" "file_sha256" "15804d37d758b6bbacaa2be8fc51b62cf1efd972fcbc52534e7ab0fff05a5402"} {"question" "q-123127" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "10{\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{2}+t-2)\\text{d}t"] ["math" "2{\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}+t-2)\\text{d}t"] ["math" "\\sqrt{10}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{2}+t-2)\\text{d}t"] ["math" "\\sqrt{10}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}-t+2)\\text{d}t"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}-t+2)\\text{d}t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/state.json" "file_sha256" "15804d37d758b6bbacaa2be8fc51b62cf1efd972fcbc52534e7ab0fff05a5402"} {"question" "q-123127" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{10}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{2}+t-2)\\text{d}t"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/state.json" "file_sha256" "15804d37d758b6bbacaa2be8fc51b62cf1efd972fcbc52534e7ab0fff05a5402"} {"question" "q-123279" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=2x+y,$ traversed along the line segment from $(-3,1)$ to $(2,3)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/state.json" "file_sha256" "15804d37d758b6bbacaa2be8fc51b62cf1efd972fcbc52534e7ab0fff05a5402"} {"question" "q-123279" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(-3,1)$ to $(2,3).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=-3\\,i+j$ and $b=2\\,i+3\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(-3\\,i+j)+t[(2\\,i+3\\,j)-(-3\\,i+j)] \\\\  & =(-3\\,i+j)+t(5\\,i+2\\,j) \\\\  & =(5t-3)\\,i+(1+2t)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=5t-3$ and $y=1+2t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5t-3)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+2t)j \\\\  & =5\\,i+2\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{29}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=2x+y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2(5t-3)+(1+2t) \\\\  & =12t-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(12t-5)\\cdot \\sqrt{29}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{29}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(12t-5)\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/state.json" "file_sha256" "15804d37d758b6bbacaa2be8fc51b62cf1efd972fcbc52534e7ab0fff05a5402"} {"question" "q-123279" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c1adca5610002853d484ecc9e3d63043a2df88e9516d60c468a45c409ff532ac"} {"question" "q-123279" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/state.json" "file_sha256" "15804d37d758b6bbacaa2be8fc51b62cf1efd972fcbc52534e7ab0fff05a5402"} {"question" "q-123279" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "29{\\int }_{0}^{1}(10t-3)\\text{d}t"] ["math" "3\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(12t-5)\\text{d}t"] ["math" "3\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(8t+7)\\text{d}t"] ["math" "\\sqrt{29}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(12t-5)\\text{d}t"] ["math" "\\sqrt{29}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(8t+7)\\text{d}t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/state.json" "file_sha256" "15804d37d758b6bbacaa2be8fc51b62cf1efd972fcbc52534e7ab0fff05a5402"} {"question" "q-123279" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{29}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(12t-5)\\text{d}t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/state.json" "file_sha256" "15804d37d758b6bbacaa2be8fc51b62cf1efd972fcbc52534e7ab0fff05a5402"} {"question" "q-76605" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi (y-x)}{8})\\,\\text{d}s$ along the line segment from $(1,1)$ to $(2,4).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/state.json" "file_sha256" "15804d37d758b6bbacaa2be8fc51b62cf1efd972fcbc52534e7ab0fff05a5402"} {"question" "q-76605" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(1,1)$ to $(2,4).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=i+j$ and $b=2\\,i+4\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(i+j)+t[2\\,i+4\\,j-(i+j)] \\\\  & =(i+j)+t(i+3\\,j) \\\\  & =(1+t)\\,i+(1+3t)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=1+t$ and $y=1+3t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+3t)j \\\\  & =1\\,i+3\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi (y-x)}{8})$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & ={sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi ((1+3t)-(1+t))}{8}) \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi t}{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi (y-x)}{8})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi t}{4})\\cdot \\sqrt{10}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi t}{4})\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=\\frac{\\pi t}{4},$ $\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{10}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi t}{4})\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{10}}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{sec}^{2}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{10}}{\\pi }\\operatorname{tan}⁡u\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{10}}{\\pi }(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{10}}{\\pi }(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{10}}{\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/state.json" "file_sha256" "15804d37d758b6bbacaa2be8fc51b62cf1efd972fcbc52534e7ab0fff05a5402"} {"question" "q-76605" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c1adca5610002853d484ecc9e3d63043a2df88e9516d60c468a45c409ff532ac"} {"question" "q-76605" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/state.json" "file_sha256" "15804d37d758b6bbacaa2be8fc51b62cf1efd972fcbc52534e7ab0fff05a5402"} {"question" "q-76605" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{4\\sqrt{10}}{\\pi }"] ["math" "\\frac{4}{\\pi }"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{10}}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{\\pi }"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/state.json" "file_sha256" "15804d37d758b6bbacaa2be8fc51b62cf1efd972fcbc52534e7ab0fff05a5402"} {"question" "q-76605" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{4\\sqrt{10}}{\\pi }"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/state.json" "file_sha256" "15804d37d758b6bbacaa2be8fc51b62cf1efd972fcbc52534e7ab0fff05a5402"} {"question" "q-123363" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=x+\\sqrt{2y}$ along the line segment from $(2,0)$ to $(2,2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/state.json" "file_sha256" "15804d37d758b6bbacaa2be8fc51b62cf1efd972fcbc52534e7ab0fff05a5402"} {"question" "q-123363" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(2,0)$ to $(2,2).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=2\\,i$ and $b=2\\,i+2\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =2\\,i+t[(2\\,i+2\\,j)-2\\,i] \\\\  & =2\\,i+2t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=2$ and $y=2t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)j \\\\  & =2j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x+\\sqrt{2y}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2+\\sqrt{2(2t)} \\\\  & =2+2\\sqrt{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2+2\\sqrt{t})\\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1}(1+\\sqrt{t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{[t+\\frac{2{t}^{3/2}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =4(1+\\frac{2}{3})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/state.json" "file_sha256" "15804d37d758b6bbacaa2be8fc51b62cf1efd972fcbc52534e7ab0fff05a5402"} {"question" "q-123363" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c1adca5610002853d484ecc9e3d63043a2df88e9516d60c468a45c409ff532ac"} {"question" "q-123363" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/state.json" "file_sha256" "15804d37d758b6bbacaa2be8fc51b62cf1efd972fcbc52534e7ab0fff05a5402"} {"question" "q-123363" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "3"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "\\frac{13}{6}"] ["math" "\\frac{17}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{20}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/state.json" "file_sha256" "15804d37d758b6bbacaa2be8fc51b62cf1efd972fcbc52534e7ab0fff05a5402"} {"question" "q-123363" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{20}{3}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/state.json" "file_sha256" "15804d37d758b6bbacaa2be8fc51b62cf1efd972fcbc52534e7ab0fff05a5402"} {"question" "q-76650" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}(4x+2)\\,{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}s$ along the line segment from $(0,1)$ to $(2,3).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/state.json" "file_sha256" "15804d37d758b6bbacaa2be8fc51b62cf1efd972fcbc52534e7ab0fff05a5402"} {"question" "q-76650" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(0,1)$ to $(2,3).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=j$ and $b=2\\,i+3\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =j+t[2\\,i+3\\,j-j] \\\\  & =j+t(2\\,i+2\\,j) \\\\  & =2t\\,i+(1+2t)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=2t$ and $y=1+2t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+2t)j \\\\  & =2\\,i+2\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=(4x+2)\\,{e}^{xy}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(4(2t)+2){e}^{2t(1+2t)} \\\\  & =(8t+2)\\,{e}^{2t+4{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(8t+2)\\,{e}^{2t+4{t}^{2}}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(8t+2)\\,{e}^{2t+4{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=2t+4{t}^{2},$ $\\text{d}u=(2+8t)\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(8t+2)\\,{e}^{2t+4{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t & =2\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{6}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\,{e}^{u}\\,{|}_{0}^{6} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}({e}^{6}-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}({e}^{6}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/state.json" "file_sha256" "15804d37d758b6bbacaa2be8fc51b62cf1efd972fcbc52534e7ab0fff05a5402"} {"question" "q-76650" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c1adca5610002853d484ecc9e3d63043a2df88e9516d60c468a45c409ff532ac"} {"question" "q-76650" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/state.json" "file_sha256" "15804d37d758b6bbacaa2be8fc51b62cf1efd972fcbc52534e7ab0fff05a5402"} {"question" "q-76650" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "({e}^{6}-1)"] ["math" "2({e}^{6}-1)"] ["math" "2\\sqrt{2}({e}^{6}-1)"] ["math" "2e\\sqrt{2}({e}^{5}-1)"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2}({e}^{6}-1)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/state.json" "file_sha256" "15804d37d758b6bbacaa2be8fc51b62cf1efd972fcbc52534e7ab0fff05a5402"} {"question" "q-76650" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\sqrt{2}({e}^{6}-1)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/state.json" "file_sha256" "15804d37d758b6bbacaa2be8fc51b62cf1efd972fcbc52534e7ab0fff05a5402"} {"question" "q-123366" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)={e}^{3x+y}$ along the line segment from $(0,0)$ to $(-1,4).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/state.json" "file_sha256" "15804d37d758b6bbacaa2be8fc51b62cf1efd972fcbc52534e7ab0fff05a5402"} {"question" "q-123366" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(0,0)$ to $(-1,4).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=0$ and $b=-i+4\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =0+t\\,(b-0) \\\\  & =t\\,(b) \\\\  & =t(-i+4\\,j) \\\\  & =-t\\,i+4t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=-t$ and $y=4t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t)j \\\\  & =-i+4\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{17}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={e}^{3x+y}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & ={e}^{3(-t)+4t} \\\\  & ={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\cdot \\sqrt{17}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{17}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{17}[{e}^{t}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{17}(e-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/state.json" "file_sha256" "15804d37d758b6bbacaa2be8fc51b62cf1efd972fcbc52534e7ab0fff05a5402"} {"question" "q-123366" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c1adca5610002853d484ecc9e3d63043a2df88e9516d60c468a45c409ff532ac"} {"question" "q-123366" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/state.json" "file_sha256" "15804d37d758b6bbacaa2be8fc51b62cf1efd972fcbc52534e7ab0fff05a5402"} {"question" "q-123366" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "4(e-1)"] ["math" "4({e}^{2}-1)"] ["math" "4e-1"] ["math" "\\sqrt{17}(e-1)"] ["math" "\\sqrt{17}({e}^{2}-1)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/state.json" "file_sha256" "15804d37d758b6bbacaa2be8fc51b62cf1efd972fcbc52534e7ab0fff05a5402"} {"question" "q-123366" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{17}(e-1)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/state.json" "file_sha256" "15804d37d758b6bbacaa2be8fc51b62cf1efd972fcbc52534e7ab0fff05a5402"} {"question" "q-76564" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=xy$ along the line segment from $(1,2)$ to $(4,7).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/state.json" "file_sha256" "15804d37d758b6bbacaa2be8fc51b62cf1efd972fcbc52534e7ab0fff05a5402"} {"question" "q-76564" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(1,2)$ to $(4,7).$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=i+2\\,j$ and $b=4\\,i+7j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(i+2\\,j)+t[4\\,i+7j-(i+2\\,j)] \\\\  & =(i+2\\,j)+t(3i+5j) \\\\  & =(1+3t)\\,i+(2+5t)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=1+3t$ and $y=2+5t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+3t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2+5t)j \\\\  & =3i+5j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{34}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=xy$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(1+3t)(2+5t) \\\\  & =15{t}^{2}+11t+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(15{t}^{2}+11t+2)\\cdot \\sqrt{34}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{34}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(15{t}^{2}+11t+2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{34}{[5{t}^{3}+\\frac{11}{2}{t}^{2}+2t]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{34}(5+\\frac{11}{2}+2)-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{25\\sqrt{34}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/state.json" "file_sha256" "15804d37d758b6bbacaa2be8fc51b62cf1efd972fcbc52534e7ab0fff05a5402"} {"question" "q-76564" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c1adca5610002853d484ecc9e3d63043a2df88e9516d60c468a45c409ff532ac"} {"question" "q-76564" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/state.json" "file_sha256" "15804d37d758b6bbacaa2be8fc51b62cf1efd972fcbc52534e7ab0fff05a5402"} {"question" "q-76564" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "25\\sqrt{34}"] ["math" "\\frac{25\\sqrt{34}}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{5\\sqrt{34}}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{34}}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/state.json" "file_sha256" "15804d37d758b6bbacaa2be8fc51b62cf1efd972fcbc52534e7ab0fff05a5402"} {"question" "q-76564" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{25\\sqrt{34}}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/state.json" "file_sha256" "15804d37d758b6bbacaa2be8fc51b62cf1efd972fcbc52534e7ab0fff05a5402"} {"question" "q-76569" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s$ along the line segment from $(1,2,0)$ to $(2,1,3).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/state.json" "file_sha256" "15804d37d758b6bbacaa2be8fc51b62cf1efd972fcbc52534e7ab0fff05a5402"} {"question" "q-76569" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from$(1,2,0)$ to $(2,1,3).$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=i+2\\,j$ and $b=2\\,i+j+3\\,k.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(i+2\\,j)+t[(2\\,i+j+3\\,k)-(i+2\\,j)] \\\\  & =(i+2\\,j)+t(i-j+3\\,k) \\\\  & =(1+t)\\,i+(2-t)\\,j+3t\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=1+t,$ $y=2-t,$ and $z=3t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2-t)j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t)k \\\\  & =i-j+3\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{11}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(1+t)(2-t)^{2} \\\\  & ={t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}+4)\\cdot \\sqrt{11}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{11}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}+4)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{11}{[\\frac{{t}^{4}}{4}-{t}^{3}+4t]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{11}(\\frac{1}{4}-1+4)-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{13\\sqrt{11}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/state.json" "file_sha256" "15804d37d758b6bbacaa2be8fc51b62cf1efd972fcbc52534e7ab0fff05a5402"} {"question" "q-76569" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c1adca5610002853d484ecc9e3d63043a2df88e9516d60c468a45c409ff532ac"} {"question" "q-76569" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/state.json" "file_sha256" "15804d37d758b6bbacaa2be8fc51b62cf1efd972fcbc52534e7ab0fff05a5402"} {"question" "q-76569" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "13\\sqrt{11}"] ["math" "\\frac{13\\sqrt{11}}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{13}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/state.json" "file_sha256" "15804d37d758b6bbacaa2be8fc51b62cf1efd972fcbc52534e7ab0fff05a5402"} {"question" "q-76569" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{13\\sqrt{11}}{4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/state.json" "file_sha256" "15804d37d758b6bbacaa2be8fc51b62cf1efd972fcbc52534e7ab0fff05a5402"} {"question" "q-123387" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{2x+y-z},$ traversed along the line segment from $(3,-2,3)$ to $(5,-1,7)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/state.json" "file_sha256" "15804d37d758b6bbacaa2be8fc51b62cf1efd972fcbc52534e7ab0fff05a5402"} {"question" "q-123387" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(3,-2,3)$ to $(5,-1,7).$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=3i-2\\,j+3k$ and $b=5\\,i-j+7\\,k.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(3i-2\\,j+3k)+t[(5\\,i-j+7\\,k)-(3i-2\\,j+3k)] \\\\  & =(3i-2\\,j+3k)+t(2i+\\,j+4k) \\\\  & =(2t+3)\\,i+(t-2)\\,j+(4t+3)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=2t+3,y=t-2,$ and $z=4t+3$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t+3)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-2)j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t+3)k \\\\  & =2i+j+4\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{21}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{2x+y-z}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\sqrt{2(2t+3)+(t-2)-(4t+3)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{t+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{t+1}\\cdot \\sqrt{21}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{21}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{t+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/state.json" "file_sha256" "15804d37d758b6bbacaa2be8fc51b62cf1efd972fcbc52534e7ab0fff05a5402"} {"question" "q-123387" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c1adca5610002853d484ecc9e3d63043a2df88e9516d60c468a45c409ff532ac"} {"question" "q-123387" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/state.json" "file_sha256" "15804d37d758b6bbacaa2be8fc51b62cf1efd972fcbc52534e7ab0fff05a5402"} {"question" "q-123387" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{5t-3}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "2\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}t\\sqrt{t-1}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "\\sqrt{21}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "\\sqrt{21}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{t+1}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "\\sqrt{21}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/state.json" "file_sha256" "15804d37d758b6bbacaa2be8fc51b62cf1efd972fcbc52534e7ab0fff05a5402"} {"question" "q-123387" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{21}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{t+1}\\,\\text{d}t"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/state.json" "file_sha256" "15804d37d758b6bbacaa2be8fc51b62cf1efd972fcbc52534e7ab0fff05a5402"} {"question" "q-76577" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}(x+z))\\,\\text{d}s$ along the line segment from $(-1,2,1)$ to $(1,2,5).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/state.json" "file_sha256" "15804d37d758b6bbacaa2be8fc51b62cf1efd972fcbc52534e7ab0fff05a5402"} {"question" "q-76577" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(-1,2,1)$ to $(1,2,5).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=-i+2\\,j+k$ and $b=i+2\\,j+5\\,k.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(-i+2\\,j+k)+t[(i+2\\,j+5\\,k)-(-i+2\\,j+k)] \\\\  & =(-i+2\\,j+k)+t(2\\,i+4\\,k) \\\\  & =(-1+2t)\\,i+2\\,j+(1+4t)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=-1+2t,$ $y=2,$ and $z=1+4t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-1+2t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2)j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+4t)k \\\\  & =2\\,i+0+4\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{20} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}(x+z))$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}((-1+2t)+(1+4t))) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi t}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}(x+z))\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi t}{2})\\cdot 2\\sqrt{5}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi t}{2})\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=\\frac{3\\pi t}{2}$, $\\text{d}u=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi t}{2})\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{3\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{3\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,{|}_{0}^{3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{3\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{3\\pi }(-1-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{3\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/state.json" "file_sha256" "15804d37d758b6bbacaa2be8fc51b62cf1efd972fcbc52534e7ab0fff05a5402"} {"question" "q-76577" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c1adca5610002853d484ecc9e3d63043a2df88e9516d60c468a45c409ff532ac"} {"question" "q-76577" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/state.json" "file_sha256" "15804d37d758b6bbacaa2be8fc51b62cf1efd972fcbc52534e7ab0fff05a5402"} {"question" "q-76577" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3\\pi }"] ["math" "-\\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{5\\pi }"] ["math" "-\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{3\\pi }"] ["math" "\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{\\pi }"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3\\pi }"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/state.json" "file_sha256" "15804d37d758b6bbacaa2be8fc51b62cf1efd972fcbc52534e7ab0fff05a5402"} {"question" "q-76577" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{3\\pi }"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/state.json" "file_sha256" "15804d37d758b6bbacaa2be8fc51b62cf1efd972fcbc52534e7ab0fff05a5402"} {"question" "q-123434" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}x{e}^{yz}\\,\\text{d}s$ along the line segment from $(2,-2,4)$ to $(4,-1,5).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/state.json" "file_sha256" "15804d37d758b6bbacaa2be8fc51b62cf1efd972fcbc52534e7ab0fff05a5402"} {"question" "q-123434" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(2,-2,4)$ to $(4,-1,5).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=2\\,i-2\\,j+4\\,k$ and $b=4\\,i-\\,j+5\\,k.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(2\\,i-2\\,j+4\\,k)+t[(4\\,i-\\,j+5\\,k)-(2\\,i-2\\,j+4\\,k)] \\\\  & =(2\\,i-2\\,j+4\\,k)+t(2\\,i+j+k) \\\\  & =(2t+2)\\,i+(t-2)\\,j+(t+4)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=2t+2,$ $y=t-2,$ and $z=t+4$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t+2)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-2)j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+4)k \\\\  & =2\\,i+j+k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y,z)=x{e}^{yz}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(2t+2){e}^{(t-2)(t+4)} \\\\  & =(2t+2){e}^{{t}^{2}+2t-8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x{e}^{yz}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t+2){e}^{{t}^{2}+2t-8}\\cdot \\sqrt{6}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t+2){e}^{{t}^{2}+2t-8}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u={t}^{2}+2t-8$, $\\text{d}u=(2t+2)\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t+2){e}^{{t}^{2}+2t-8}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\sqrt{6}\\,{\\int }_{-8}^{-5}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}\\,{e}^{u}\\,{|}_{-8}^{-5} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}({e}^{-5}-{e}^{-8}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}(\\frac{1}{{e}^{5}}-\\frac{1}{{e}^{8}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}({e}^{3}-1)}{{e}^{8}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/state.json" "file_sha256" "15804d37d758b6bbacaa2be8fc51b62cf1efd972fcbc52534e7ab0fff05a5402"} {"question" "q-123434" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c1adca5610002853d484ecc9e3d63043a2df88e9516d60c468a45c409ff532ac"} {"question" "q-123434" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/state.json" "file_sha256" "15804d37d758b6bbacaa2be8fc51b62cf1efd972fcbc52534e7ab0fff05a5402"} {"question" "q-123434" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}({e}^{3}+2)}{{e}^{8}}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}({e}^{2}-1)}{{e}^{3}}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}({e}^{3}-1)}{{e}^{8}}"] ["math" "\\frac{{e}^{3}+1}{{e}^{5}}"] ["math" "\\frac{{e}^{3}-1}{{e}^{8}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/state.json" "file_sha256" "15804d37d758b6bbacaa2be8fc51b62cf1efd972fcbc52534e7ab0fff05a5402"} {"question" "q-123434" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}({e}^{3}-1)}{{e}^{8}}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/state.json" "file_sha256" "15804d37d758b6bbacaa2be8fc51b62cf1efd972fcbc52534e7ab0fff05a5402"} {"question" "q-123431" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{y+z}{x},$ traversed along the line segment from $(-4,1,5)$ to $(-2,3,3)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/state.json" "file_sha256" "15804d37d758b6bbacaa2be8fc51b62cf1efd972fcbc52534e7ab0fff05a5402"} {"question" "q-123431" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(-4,1,5)$ to $(-2,3,3).$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=-4i+j+5\\,k$ and $b=-2\\,i+3\\,j+3\\,k.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(-4i+j+5\\,k)+t[(-2\\,i+3\\,j+3\\,k)-(-4i+j+5\\,k)] \\\\  & =(-4i+j+5\\,k)+t(2i+2\\,j-2\\,k) \\\\  & =(2t-4)\\,i+(2t+1)\\,j+(5-2t)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=2t-4,y=2t+1,$ and $z=5-2t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-4)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t+1)j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5-2t)k \\\\  & =2\\,i+2\\,j-2\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{12} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{y+z}{x}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{(2t+1)+(5-2t)}{2t-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{2t-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{t-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{3}{t-2}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{t-2}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/state.json" "file_sha256" "15804d37d758b6bbacaa2be8fc51b62cf1efd972fcbc52534e7ab0fff05a5402"} {"question" "q-123431" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c1adca5610002853d484ecc9e3d63043a2df88e9516d60c468a45c409ff532ac"} {"question" "q-123431" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/state.json" "file_sha256" "15804d37d758b6bbacaa2be8fc51b62cf1efd972fcbc52534e7ab0fff05a5402"} {"question" "q-123431" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{2t-1}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "2\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{t}{t-2}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "6\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{t-2}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "6\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+2}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "6\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2t+1}{t-1}\\,\\text{d}t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/state.json" "file_sha256" "15804d37d758b6bbacaa2be8fc51b62cf1efd972fcbc52534e7ab0fff05a5402"} {"question" "q-123431" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "6\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{t-2}\\,\\text{d}t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/state.json" "file_sha256" "15804d37d758b6bbacaa2be8fc51b62cf1efd972fcbc52534e7ab0fff05a5402"} {"question" "e-9486" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a definite integral that is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y) = x+\\sin{y},$ traversed along the line segment from $(1,0)$ to $(1,1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/example-9486.json" "file_sha256" "333396975d81631906a3636701387f003315389c4293a52da34c29ee90753245"} {"question" "e-9486" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s  =  \\int\\limits_{t_a}^{t_b}  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(1,0)$ to $(1,1).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $\\mathbf{a}=\\mathbf{i}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =i+t[(i+j)-i] \\\\  & =i+t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0 \\le t \\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=1$ and $y=t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =0\\,i+j \\\\  & =j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y) = x+\\sin{y}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/example-9486.json" "file_sha256" "333396975d81631906a3636701387f003315389c4293a52da34c29ee90753245"} {"question" "e-6304" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y) = e^{x+y}$ along the line segment from $(0,3)$ to $(2,3).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/example-6304.json" "file_sha256" "d12a0b88e9030fe48f49895239ab22f6ebe8d83b41e6530d91e2c137783d57d3"} {"question" "e-6304" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s  = \\int\\limits_{t_a}^{t_b}  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(0,3)$ to $(2,3).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $\\mathbf{a}=3\\,\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=2\\,\\mathbf{i}+3\\,\\mathbf{j}.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =3\\,j+t[(2\\,i+3\\,j)-3\\,j] \\\\  & =3\\,j+t(2\\,i) \\\\  & =2t\\,i+3\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0 \\le t \\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=2t$ and $y=3$ along the segment.\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3)j \\\\  & =2\\,i \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y) = e^{x+y},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & ={e}^{2t+3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{2t+3}\\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{2t+3}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=2t+3,$ $\\textrm d u=2\\,\\textrm d t$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{2t+3}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{3}^{5}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\,{|}_{3}^{5} \\\\  & ={e}^{5}-{e}^{3} \\\\  & ={e}^{3}({e}^{2}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/example-6304.json" "file_sha256" "d12a0b88e9030fe48f49895239ab22f6ebe8d83b41e6530d91e2c137783d57d3"} {"question" "e-9487" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the line integral of the function $f(x,y,z) = x^2+y-z,$ traversed along the line segment from $(0,1,-3)$ to $(-1,2,-2)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/example-9487.json" "file_sha256" "e2d2f8fb4a8471e5d2d8f15df35450d7b97198ea6f022fb5d23900cd98862c9b"} {"question" "e-9487" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}s  =  \\int\\limits_{t_a}^{t_b}  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(0,1,-3)$ to $(-1,2,-2).$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $\\mathbf{a}=\\mathbf{j}-3\\mathbf{k}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=-\\,\\mathbf{i}+2\\,\\mathbf{j}-2\\,\\mathbf{k}.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(j-3k)+t[(-\\,i+2\\,j-2\\,k)-(j-3k)] \\\\  & =(j-3k)+t(-i+\\,j+k) \\\\  & =-t\\,i+(t+1)\\,j+(t-3)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0 \\le t \\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=-t, y=t+1,$ and $z=t-3$ along the segment.\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+1)j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-3)k \\\\  & =-i+j+k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y) = x^2+y-z$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(-t)^{2}+(t+1)-(t-3) \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}+4)\\cdot \\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}+4)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14058331/example-9487.json" "file_sha256" "e2d2f8fb4a8471e5d2d8f15df35450d7b97198ea6f022fb5d23900cd98862c9b"}] "usage" {"e-6304" [] "q-123269" [] "e-9487" [] "e-9486" [] "q-123127" []} "basis" 1868 "retractions" [[17592186179917 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x, y) = \\ln (x + y)$, traversed along the line segment from $(1, 3)$ to $(0, 3)$?"] [17592186179912 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x, y) = x^{2} - y$, traversed along the line segment from $(0, 2)$ to $(3, 1)$?"] [17592186179516 :question/problem "Find a definite integral that is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y) = x+\\sin{y}$, traversed along the line segment from $(1,0)$ to $(1,1)$."] [17592186179516 :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_{t_a}^{t_b} f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is line segment from $(1,0)$ to $(1,1)$.\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}=\\mathbf{i}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{b}=\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nSo, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(t) &= a + t(b - a) \\\\\n&= i + t[(i + j) - i] \\\\\n&= i + tj\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n0 \\le t \\le 1\n$$\n\nThus, we have that $x=1$ and $y=t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\frac{dx}{dt}i + \\frac{dy}{dt}j \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(1)i + \\frac{d}{dt}(t)j \\\\\n&= 0i + j \\\\\n&= j \\\\\n‖r^{′}(t)‖ &= \\sqrt{(\\frac{dx}{dt})^{2} + (\\frac{dy}{dt})^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{0^{2} + 1^{2}} \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = x+\\sin{y}\n$$\n\n, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(r(t)) &= 1 + \\sin t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_(C)f(x, y)ds &= ∫_{0}^{1}f(r(t))‖r^{′}(t)‖dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}(1 + \\sin t) \\cdot 1 \\cdot dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}(1 + \\sin t)dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186179455 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y) = e^{x+y}$ along the line segment from $(0,3)$ to $(2,3)$."] [17592186179455 :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_{t_a}^{t_b} f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is line segment from $(0,3)$ to $(2,3)$.\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}=3\\,\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{b}=2\\,\\mathbf{i}+3\\,\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nSo, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(t) &= a + t(b - a) \\\\\n&= 3j + t[(2i + 3j) - 3j] \\\\\n&= 3j + t(2i) \\\\\n&= 2ti + 3j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n0 \\le t \\le 1\n$$\n\nThus, we have that $x=2t$ and $y=3$ along the segment.\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\frac{dx}{dt}i + \\frac{dy}{dt}j \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(2t)i + \\frac{d}{dt}(3)j \\\\\n&= 2i \\\\\n‖r^{′}(t)‖ &= \\sqrt{(\\frac{dx}{dt})^{2} + (\\frac{dy}{dt})^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2^{2} + 0^{2}} \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = e^{x+y}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(r(t)) &= e^{2t + 3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_(C)e^{x + y}ds &= ∫_{0}^{1}f(r(t))‖r^{′}(t)‖dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}e^{2t + 3} \\cdot 2 \\cdot dt \\\\\n&= 2∫_{0}^{1}e^{2t + 3}dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution\n\n$$\nu=2t+3\n$$\n\n$$\n\\textrm d u=2\\,\\textrm d t\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2∫_{0}^{1}e^{2t + 3}dt &= ∫_{3}^{5}e^{u}du \\\\\n&= e^{u} \\mid _{3}^{5} \\\\\n&= e^{5} - e^{3} \\\\\n&= e^{3}(e^{2} - 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186179517 :question/problem "Find the line integral of the function $f(x,y,z) = x^2+y-z$, traversed along the line segment from $(0,1,-3)$ to $(-1,2,-2)$?"] [17592186179517 :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_{t_a}^{t_b} f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is line segment from $(0,1,-3)$ to $(-1,2,-2)$.\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}=\\mathbf{j}-3\\mathbf{k}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{b}=-\\,\\mathbf{i}+2\\,\\mathbf{j}-2\\,\\mathbf{k}\n$$\n\nSo, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(t) &= a + t(b - a) \\\\\n&= (j - 3k) + t[(-i + 2j - 2k) - (j - 3k)] \\\\\n&= (j - 3k) + t(-i + j + k) \\\\\n&=-ti + (t + 1)j + (t - 3)k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n0 \\le t \\le 1\n$$\n\nThus, we have that\n\n$$\nx=-t, y=t+1\n$$\n\nand $z=t-3$ along the segment.\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(u) &= \\frac{dx}{dt}i + \\frac{dy}{dt}j + \\frac{dz}{dt}k \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(-t)i + \\frac{d}{dt}(t + 1)j + \\frac{d}{dt}(t - 3)k \\\\\n&=-i + j + k \\\\\n‖r^{′}(t)‖ &= \\sqrt{(\\frac{dx}{dt})^{2} + (\\frac{dy}{dt})^{2} + (\\frac{dz}{dt})^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(-1)^{2} + 1^{2} + 1^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = x^2+y-z\n$$\n\n, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(r(t)) &= (-t)^{2} + (t + 1) - (t - 3) \\\\\n&= t^{2} + 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_(C)f(x, y)ds &= ∫_{0}^{1}f(r(t))‖r^{′}(t)‖dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}(t^{2} + 4) \\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\cdot dt \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{3}∫_{0}^{1}(t^{2} + 4)dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186177473 17592186177474 17592186177475 17592186177476 17592186177477 17592186177503 17592186177504 17592186177505 17592186177506 17592186177507] "immutable_fields" [[17592186177472 :answer-field/correct] [17592186177472 :answer-field/id] [17592186177472 :answer-field/key] [17592186177472 :answer-field/type] [17592186177502 :answer-field/correct] [17592186177502 :answer-field/id] [17592186177502 :answer-field/key] [17592186177502 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
